00:00:59.167 --> 00:01:01.290
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We're back to 100%.

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:04.891
&gt;&gt; So Ruth, it looks like we have
some beta testers for you here today,

00:01:04.891 --> 00:01:05.847
if you need them.

00:01:05.847 --> 00:01:06.799
&gt;&gt; Perfect.

00:01:06.799 --> 00:01:10.652
&gt;&gt; There's so much to cover, from your
early beginnings, to Morgan Stanley,

00:01:10.652 --> 00:01:12.088
to now Alphabet and Google.

00:01:12.088 --> 00:01:13.799
So let's jump in.

00:01:13.799 --> 00:01:20.150
Before your parents landed here in Silicon
Valley, they had a very difficult journey.

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:24.660
And your father fled, escaped with his
life from Austria in World War II.

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:27.750
How has your family history
shaped your values?

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:34.114
&gt;&gt; Well, probably the most important thing
my father always said is that education

00:01:34.114 --> 00:01:35.771
is your passport for life.

00:01:35.771 --> 00:01:39.210
And the reason he said that
was very much to your point.

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:41.909
He fled Austria having been
thrown out of high school.

00:01:41.909 --> 00:01:47.490
He was Jewish, and was fortunate
to be one of the few to get out.

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:49.040
His family did not.

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:53.330
He was young enough that he was on one
of the youth boats, went to Palestine.

00:01:53.330 --> 00:01:56.535
And as soon as he could,
he enlisted in the British army.

00:01:56.535 --> 00:02:00.830
And while he was actually fighting in the
British army, it occurred to him that if

00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:04.872
he ever wanted to live in a place where
there was freedom for him and hopefully

00:02:04.872 --> 00:02:09.068
a family that he would one day have,
he needed a skill that people would value.

00:02:09.068 --> 00:02:11.352
And he concluded that
that would be physics.

00:02:11.352 --> 00:02:15.200
And so somehow while fighting in
the British army he started to

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:16.760
teach himself physics.

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:19.843
He sold the cigarettes and somehow got
material to teach himself physics.

00:02:19.843 --> 00:02:22.159
And his fellow soldiers
would tease him and say,

00:02:22.159 --> 00:02:24.900
you're going to die before
you can ever use this.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:27.489
And his answer was always,
I would rather die an educated man.

00:02:27.489 --> 00:02:31.340
And he told us that story frequently.

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:34.584
And it made it hard if we didn't do really
well in school, because I didn't even

00:02:34.584 --> 00:02:37.435
have the benefit of all this stuff,
so you should do really well.

00:02:37.435 --> 00:02:39.845
[LAUGH] But
it was a really important value.

00:02:39.845 --> 00:02:44.621
One, the importance of education, and two,
the gratitude to be in a country where you

00:02:44.621 --> 00:02:47.710
do have freedoms that so
many people don't have.

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:49.349
So those were probably the most profound.

00:02:49.349 --> 00:02:51.899
My mother was amazing, a psychologist.

00:02:51.899 --> 00:02:54.701
And I remember as a very young girl,
I was born in England.

00:02:54.701 --> 00:02:58.331
And I'll never forget the day,
I was eight, my sister was six.

00:02:58.331 --> 00:03:01.389
I know that because it was shortly
before we moved back to California.

00:03:01.389 --> 00:03:05.144
And she said, it's very important
as a woman to have a career.

00:03:05.144 --> 00:03:08.322
She's telling an eight-year-old and
a six-year-old that,

00:03:08.322 --> 00:03:11.798
which she thought was a perfectly
normal time to give me career advice.

00:03:11.798 --> 00:03:12.749
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:03:12.749 --> 00:03:15.544
she made the point that every
cent she made went to our nanny.

00:03:15.544 --> 00:03:19.201
She worked, and I think there was
part of her that felt this guilt

00:03:19.201 --> 00:03:21.860
that many of us feel that
she was working a lot.

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:25.829
But she was also talking about
the gratification she got and

00:03:25.829 --> 00:03:29.250
what it meant for
her to be able to work with others.

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:30.750
And why it was important
to find one's passion.

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:33.260
So I think they very much shaped who I am.

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:37.559
And I'm eternally grateful to them.

00:03:37.559 --> 00:03:41.081
&gt;&gt; You took a commitment to education and
career, of course, seriously.

00:03:41.081 --> 00:03:43.507
You graduated with three degrees.

00:03:43.507 --> 00:03:47.369
In 1987, when you graduated
from Wharton with your MBA,

00:03:47.369 --> 00:03:51.232
you accepted an offer at
Morgan Stanley to join the mergers and

00:03:51.232 --> 00:03:54.522
acquisitions group,
the hottest role at the time.

00:03:54.522 --> 00:03:57.580
Within a few weeks of joining,
the stock market declined,

00:03:57.580 --> 00:04:00.102
the biggest decline in
Wall Street's history.

00:04:00.102 --> 00:04:02.602
How did that shape your experience?

00:04:02.602 --> 00:04:06.800
Or what did you learn
from that experience?

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:08.136
&gt;&gt; Well, that's pretty terrifying.

00:04:08.136 --> 00:04:10.730
It started in August of 87 and
October of 87,

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:13.530
on a percentage basis was
the biggest decline ever.

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:17.453
I first thought my career was
over before it had even started.

00:04:17.453 --> 00:04:21.822
And when I joined Morgan Stanley, I was of
the view that I would only do mergers and

00:04:21.822 --> 00:04:22.729
acquisitions.

00:04:22.729 --> 00:04:26.121
I just knew that's what
I was interested in, and

00:04:26.121 --> 00:04:29.860
I would rather leave the firm
than do something else.

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:33.207
But of course, markets change and
opportunities change.

00:04:33.207 --> 00:04:37.717
And probably one of the most important
things is somebody I really respected,

00:04:37.717 --> 00:04:42.359
who I had worked on a deal with, said,
hey, we're going to create this new thing,

00:04:42.359 --> 00:04:45.120
it's going to be covering
private equity firms.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:46.965
It hadn't been done on Wall Street before.

00:04:46.965 --> 00:04:50.540
And at the time I thought
it was a really bad idea.

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:51.582
And why would I want to go do that?

00:04:51.582 --> 00:04:54.940
So I said I would hedge my bet and
do it half time.

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:57.935
And within no time realized it was
a really exciting thing to do.

00:04:57.935 --> 00:05:02.368
And it was the first time I realized
that if you're working with people who

00:05:02.368 --> 00:05:04.726
are really smart and take a risk on you,

00:05:04.726 --> 00:05:09.252
they may open doors that you don't even
appreciate are doors to be opened.

00:05:09.252 --> 00:05:12.720
And I didn't use the word,
sponsor back then.

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:15.728
It probably took me another decade or
two to appreciate,

00:05:15.728 --> 00:05:17.803
that's really what I was looking for.

00:05:17.803 --> 00:05:24.050
But it led me to another
place that I hadn't expected.

00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:27.799
And I think the willingness to be flexible
and adjust as the world adjusts or

00:05:27.799 --> 00:05:30.358
as you adjust or
as your life requirements adjust,

00:05:30.358 --> 00:05:32.756
was probably one of
the most important things.

00:05:32.756 --> 00:05:36.750
I didn't have an option, the world
changed, but fortunately I wasn't rigid.

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:43.249
&gt;&gt; One of the statistics that comes to
mind is that, for us, many of us are about

00:05:43.249 --> 00:05:47.720
to graduate and half of us switch
jobs within five years of graduation.

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:53.210
And you mention a sponsor or
the idea of sponsorship.

00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:58.787
And you, yourself, switched out of
Morgan Stanley to join Smith Barney,

00:05:58.787 --> 00:06:00.478
following a sponsor.

00:06:00.478 --> 00:06:04.950
And you've noted in the past that
you immediately regretted that move.

00:06:04.950 --> 00:06:05.991
So help us.

00:06:05.991 --> 00:06:09.620
How did you recognize and
fix your perceived mistake?

00:06:11.630 --> 00:06:15.142
So, I choose to say that, that period
of my life didn't actually exist, but

00:06:15.142 --> 00:06:16.559
you're taking me there, so.

00:06:16.559 --> 00:06:19.380
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Thank you for indulging.

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:24.114
&gt;&gt; So two of the people I worked really
closely with were leaving Morgan Stanley,

00:06:24.114 --> 00:06:28.417
and asked me to go and actually run
this private equity coverage group,

00:06:28.417 --> 00:06:32.770
which seemed like a wonderful step up and
it could be really exciting.

00:06:32.770 --> 00:06:34.605
But I really loved Morgan Stanley.

00:06:34.605 --> 00:06:37.977
In fact, my father said he was surprised
when I chose to leave because I loved

00:06:37.977 --> 00:06:39.359
the ethics of Morgan Stanley.

00:06:39.359 --> 00:06:43.310
That was the word that
he chose to focus on.

00:06:43.310 --> 00:06:46.370
At the time,
I was pregnant with our second child.

00:06:46.370 --> 00:06:51.166
And a managing director at Morgan Stanley
in front of me at one point had

00:06:51.166 --> 00:06:55.080
said to a client,
women may come back after their first.

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:56.810
They never come back after their second.

00:06:56.810 --> 00:07:00.311
And I was terrified that what
had been a really great career,

00:07:00.311 --> 00:07:04.235
where people were looking out for
me, might get derailed quickly.

00:07:04.235 --> 00:07:06.949
Because maybe the people
I'd been working for, men,

00:07:06.949 --> 00:07:10.560
because there were so few women back
in the day in leadership positions.

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:13.481
Maybe they got it and they had ethics, but

00:07:13.481 --> 00:07:16.914
maybe this thing I had
heard was more pervasive.

00:07:16.914 --> 00:07:23.210
And I kept hinting that I was concerned my
career wouldn't stay on the pace it was.

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:26.694
And nobody quite got the hint, nor
did they know I was pregnant, and

00:07:26.694 --> 00:07:28.602
that’s why I was really concerned.

00:07:28.602 --> 00:07:29.335
And so I left.

00:07:29.335 --> 00:07:32.777
And after a couple months, I said,
the culture is different, and

00:07:32.777 --> 00:07:35.270
I want to be back at Morgan Stanley.

00:07:35.270 --> 00:07:39.734
And I went back and
I actually spoke to the then CEO and said,

00:07:39.734 --> 00:07:42.960
here is the subtext I never shared openly.

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:45.588
Which of course, his immediate
response was, that's ridiculous,

00:07:45.588 --> 00:07:46.710
of course, we want you back.

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:53.080
And yet, because 40 people followed
after I did, the door was shut.

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:56.219
And the lesson to me,
is if you're thinking about switching,

00:07:56.219 --> 00:07:58.030
put everything out there.

00:07:58.030 --> 00:08:01.940
The worst thing that can happen is
someone will tell you, sorry no.

00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:06.948
I'm not going to honor that which
you want and you should go.

00:08:06.948 --> 00:08:08.918
But you might actually find they do.

00:08:08.918 --> 00:08:12.538
And if you find that they do,
you end up having the choice you want.

00:08:12.538 --> 00:08:14.366
And if you find that they didn't,

00:08:14.366 --> 00:08:17.089
you don't have any regrets
because at least you tried.

00:08:17.089 --> 00:08:20.451
The reason I was able to go back is
another really important lesson.

00:08:20.451 --> 00:08:24.082
Of those 40 people, I was the only one
who got the opportunity to return.

00:08:24.082 --> 00:08:28.991
And I think it really goes to this mantra
I live by which is the world is small,

00:08:28.991 --> 00:08:30.154
and life is long.

00:08:30.154 --> 00:08:34.069
And so make sure everything you do,
you do in a high quality way.

00:08:34.069 --> 00:08:39.040
And so that enabled people to say,
I should come back at a certain point.

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:40.408
And I got the call three years later.

00:08:40.408 --> 00:08:42.401
But there was so much in there.

00:08:42.401 --> 00:08:46.439
And any time someone's coming and
telling me they want to switch careers,

00:08:46.439 --> 00:08:49.273
I tell them this story and
say, put it all out there.

00:08:49.273 --> 00:08:51.368
Make sure you've left nothing unsaid.

00:08:51.368 --> 00:08:55.676
Because you might be surprised and people
might say, you know what, you're wrong and

00:08:55.676 --> 00:08:56.808
you should come here.

00:08:56.808 --> 00:08:59.811
Or here's a better opportunity here,
and you'll never regret it.

00:08:59.811 --> 00:09:03.579
&gt;&gt; Thank you, that would be helpful for
the 200 of us switching jobs.

00:09:03.579 --> 00:09:04.239
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:09:04.239 --> 00:09:05.368
&gt;&gt; In a few years time [LAUGH].

00:09:05.368 --> 00:09:09.154
So let's fast-forward a bit.

00:09:09.154 --> 00:09:10.764
It's September in 2008.

00:09:10.764 --> 00:09:15.066
By this time, you have proven yourself
at Morgan Stanley many times over,

00:09:15.066 --> 00:09:17.950
holding many of the titles
that Laura read.

00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:21.160
One of which was Vice Chair
of Investment Banking.

00:09:22.310 --> 00:09:25.370
But also the economy is tanking.

00:09:25.370 --> 00:09:30.413
Hank Paulson gives you a call, asks you to
please help him save the financial system.

00:09:30.413 --> 00:09:34.069
&gt;&gt; Wasn't quite said that way.

00:09:34.069 --> 00:09:36.955
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Lead advisor to the US Treasury.

00:09:36.955 --> 00:09:38.046
Yeah, thank you.

00:09:38.046 --> 00:09:39.376
I need your color, so please.

00:09:39.376 --> 00:09:45.743
Could you please walk us through that
experience and what you learned?

00:09:45.743 --> 00:09:51.053
&gt;&gt; Well, it was actually July of '08,
he called and said, I need some help on

00:09:51.053 --> 00:09:56.973
Fanny Mae and Freddy Mack, understanding
what's going on with the housing crisis.

00:09:56.973 --> 00:10:00.945
And so, a team and
I went down to the Treasury Departmen.

00:10:00.945 --> 00:10:05.656
And of course, July of '08, shortly
before the election, our question to him

00:10:05.656 --> 00:10:09.707
was do you want to kick the can down
the road to the next administration?

00:10:09.707 --> 00:10:11.124
Or do you want to deal with this?

00:10:11.124 --> 00:10:13.698
And his comment was I
don't think we have time.

00:10:13.698 --> 00:10:18.455
I'm concerned, there's going to be
the proverbial run on the bank.

00:10:18.455 --> 00:10:20.334
Let me understand when Fannie Mae and

00:10:20.334 --> 00:10:23.249
Freddie Mac will not be able
to fund themselves anymore.

00:10:24.410 --> 00:10:28.360
And this was a $5 trillion
balance sheet in the aggregate.

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:30.582
And his concern was were we at the brink.

00:10:30.582 --> 00:10:35.455
And he basically said analyze
every component of it.

00:10:35.455 --> 00:10:36.294
What's the funding?

00:10:36.294 --> 00:10:37.173
What's the capital?

00:10:37.173 --> 00:10:38.314
What can we do?

00:10:38.314 --> 00:10:39.259
What are the options?

00:10:39.259 --> 00:10:45.000
And that's what we did around
the clock until September.

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:49.228
Looking at what were the alternatives,
how stressed was the housing market,

00:10:49.228 --> 00:10:52.505
what did it mean, what was the risk,
what were the options.

00:10:52.505 --> 00:10:55.826
And it was extra ordinary
seeing him in action.

00:10:55.826 --> 00:10:57.930
And there were so many lessons learnt.

00:10:57.930 --> 00:11:01.330
You can't learn while you're in that, but

00:11:01.330 --> 00:11:06.220
you have to have the team that actually
has instinct based on experience.

00:11:06.220 --> 00:11:09.731
And it really informs how I think about
the type of team I pulled together.

00:11:09.731 --> 00:11:13.509
I want to have a mix of people who
are extraordinary entrepreneurial,

00:11:13.509 --> 00:11:17.797
will walk through any wall because they
don't know you're not supposed to walk

00:11:17.797 --> 00:11:18.831
through any wall.

00:11:18.831 --> 00:11:21.204
With people who have
what I call battle scars.

00:11:21.204 --> 00:11:26.096
They've seen three, four, five chapters
ahead, and it's that mix that enables you,

00:11:26.096 --> 00:11:30.190
I think, to come up with creative but
implementable solutions.

00:11:30.190 --> 00:11:33.250
And so we literally went around
the clock till September,

00:11:33.250 --> 00:11:36.700
then announced the conservatorship,

00:11:36.700 --> 00:11:41.070
as it was called, the path forward for
Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac.

00:11:41.070 --> 00:11:45.090
And went around the globe because his
concern at that point was we need to

00:11:45.090 --> 00:11:49.090
make sure every central bank
that holds U.S. treasuries.

00:11:49.090 --> 00:11:54.887
Or these securities understands that we're
really protecting their holdings in U.S.

00:11:54.887 --> 00:11:58.686
treasuries and
what's going to happen with the economy.

00:11:58.686 --> 00:12:00.060
And came back from that, and

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:03.454
that was kind of step one in what
continued to be a pretty painful fall.

00:12:03.454 --> 00:12:06.955
&gt;&gt; Why did you decide to join that team?

00:12:06.955 --> 00:12:10.846
It sounds,
you're not one to shy away from work, but

00:12:10.846 --> 00:12:14.160
it sounded particularly precarious.

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:16.680
&gt;&gt; Well there was more to it than that,

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:20.279
and it sort of goes back to your
first question about my parents.

00:12:21.470 --> 00:12:24.961
When we were asked if we
would advise the US Treasury,

00:12:24.961 --> 00:12:30.093
one of the questions I was asked by the
senior leadership team at Morgan Stein.

00:12:30.093 --> 00:12:32.859
At the time, is that if we were
not advising the government,

00:12:32.859 --> 00:12:35.314
what would we be doing for
Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac?

00:12:35.314 --> 00:12:37.713
And what would the fees be.

00:12:37.713 --> 00:12:42.071
To be quite blunt about it, you know
if we were advising Fannie Mae and

00:12:42.071 --> 00:12:45.077
Freddie Mac, and
the initial conclusion was,

00:12:45.077 --> 00:12:48.253
we probably shouldn't
take on this assignment.

00:12:48.253 --> 00:12:52.333
And I remember saying there
are certain times in history where

00:12:52.333 --> 00:12:56.505
there's only one right option,
and this is the wrong option.

00:12:56.505 --> 00:13:00.169
And fortunately, one of the people
who was still at the firm,

00:13:00.169 --> 00:13:02.930
who was extraordinary, completely agreed.

00:13:02.930 --> 00:13:06.687
The CEO had already gone out to
make a call to Hank Paulson to

00:13:06.687 --> 00:13:11.535
turn down the assignment, and
we had this second round of conversations.

00:13:11.535 --> 00:13:15.292
Fortunately, Hank did not pick up
the phone, so we came out and said,

00:13:15.292 --> 00:13:16.640
there's only one time.

00:13:16.640 --> 00:13:19.608
There are times in history there's only
one choice, and you must speak up and

00:13:19.608 --> 00:13:20.715
you must do the right thing.

00:13:20.715 --> 00:13:23.279
And my view was that
was one of those times.

00:13:23.279 --> 00:13:26.907
If we were being asked to help at
a time that was so precarious for

00:13:26.907 --> 00:13:29.988
not just the US economy,
but the global economy, and

00:13:29.988 --> 00:13:33.224
we had skills that would be relevant,
we should serve.

00:13:33.224 --> 00:13:37.630
Message there is use your voice.

00:13:37.630 --> 00:13:39.790
You're at the table because
people want your voice.

00:13:39.790 --> 00:13:42.645
And I think it's very easy
sometimes to forget that, or

00:13:42.645 --> 00:13:45.824
not be sure of your actions,
saying something the right way.

00:13:45.824 --> 00:13:48.185
The number of times I've said something,
and then I go home and I say,

00:13:48.185 --> 00:13:49.156
did I say that the right way?

00:13:49.156 --> 00:13:50.467
Maybe I could've said it better.

00:13:50.467 --> 00:13:52.267
And then you're like, you know what.

00:13:52.267 --> 00:13:53.221
Don't keep second guessing.

00:13:53.221 --> 00:13:55.509
Make sure, though,
that if you point of view,

00:13:55.509 --> 00:13:58.668
the reason you're being hired is
people want your point of view.

00:13:58.668 --> 00:14:00.285
It's a really important for me learning.

00:14:00.285 --> 00:14:02.021
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:14:02.021 --> 00:14:06.209
So moving a little bit ahead
to- there's so much to cover.

00:14:06.209 --> 00:14:06.814
&gt;&gt; Happier days.

00:14:06.814 --> 00:14:07.467
&gt;&gt; I think so.

00:14:07.467 --> 00:14:13.613
You tell me, let's see.
So February 2015, also correct my days.

00:14:13.613 --> 00:14:17.471
[LAUGH] Upon writing
the Morgan Stanley ship,

00:14:17.471 --> 00:14:21.340
you want to to serve as CFO for
five years.

00:14:21.340 --> 00:14:24.190
And you consulted
Silicon Valley executive and

00:14:24.190 --> 00:14:27.330
coach Bill Campbell,
about what you might do next.

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:32.370
You reportedly told him that you
didn't want another CFO job.

00:14:32.370 --> 00:14:34.080
&gt;&gt; Correct.

00:14:34.080 --> 00:14:35.750
I was very clear minded about that.

00:14:37.380 --> 00:14:38.505
We'll get there.

00:14:38.505 --> 00:14:44.270
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] What made the Google CFO
job different or compelling?

00:14:44.270 --> 00:14:47.211
&gt;&gt; So, you've got the chronology, right?

00:14:47.211 --> 00:14:51.936
It's sort of gone through coming out
of what was a really painful period for

00:14:51.936 --> 00:14:53.515
Morgan Stanley as well.

00:14:53.515 --> 00:14:58.500
And we worked over the Ensuing
number of years to really position

00:14:58.500 --> 00:15:01.282
Morgan Stanley I think quite well.

00:15:01.282 --> 00:15:03.896
I think the CEO James Gorman
is extraordinary and

00:15:03.896 --> 00:15:05.820
it was wonderful working with him.

00:15:05.820 --> 00:15:08.673
I sort of reached that point where I said,

00:15:08.673 --> 00:15:14.067
I don't want to look back in five years
and say why did I do that next five years?

00:15:14.067 --> 00:15:19.254
What's the next mountain that is out there
that 's going to be really exciting,

00:15:19.254 --> 00:15:20.758
that I can be proud of?

00:15:20.758 --> 00:15:27.095
And to leave Morgan Stanley and CFO there
and like, I don't want to just go do that.

00:15:27.095 --> 00:15:31.602
And so I thought I knew I didn't want to
just do that, I didn't want to be a CFO.

00:15:31.602 --> 00:15:35.066
And so I went to Bill Campbell,
an extraordinary person,

00:15:35.066 --> 00:15:38.544
book just came out about him,
the trillion dollar coach.

00:15:38.544 --> 00:15:42.540
That really gives his words of wisdom And
it was really just to get some guidance.

00:15:42.540 --> 00:15:46.520
How to think about what
that next chapter might be.

00:15:46.520 --> 00:15:48.460
And he did ask me, so
do you want to be CFO?

00:15:48.460 --> 00:15:49.100
And I'm like, no.
But

00:15:49.100 --> 00:15:51.120
the one thing I know is that
I don't want to be CFO.

00:15:51.120 --> 00:15:53.830
And he kept returning to that question.

00:15:53.830 --> 00:15:56.600
And after about two hours he
ended with that question.

00:15:56.600 --> 00:15:59.280
So the one thing you know is you don't
want to be a CFO, and I said correct.

00:15:59.280 --> 00:16:02.230
Then he said I have the perfect job,
CFO of Google.

00:16:02.230 --> 00:16:05.930
And within, literally within a second
I said well if you're serious,

00:16:05.930 --> 00:16:07.595
of course I'm interested in that.

00:16:07.595 --> 00:16:13.085
[LAUGH] And, as I told you,
when the facts change, I'm flexible.

00:16:13.085 --> 00:16:16.160
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And it hadn't even occurred to me.

00:16:16.160 --> 00:16:20.370
Google was a company that I had loved for
a really long time.

00:16:20.370 --> 00:16:25.189
First invested fortunately
through an angel fund in 1998,

00:16:25.189 --> 00:16:27.966
was part of the ITO back in 2004.

00:16:27.966 --> 00:16:30.980
Thought and
think the founders are extraordinary.

00:16:30.980 --> 00:16:36.900
The mission is really inspiring and
so I left his home

00:16:36.900 --> 00:16:41.560
still not believing it was possible,
but it's just an amazing place to be.

00:16:43.420 --> 00:16:48.671
&gt;&gt; When you joined, Google was known for
it's slogan, "Don't be evil," less so

00:16:48.671 --> 00:16:55.950
its processes, arguably,
you led the way to financial rigor.

00:16:55.950 --> 00:16:59.310
Including by having managers
set business milestones and

00:16:59.310 --> 00:17:03.880
even factor in the cost of employee
stock options into their budgets.

00:17:03.880 --> 00:17:05.250
Something they hadn't been doing before.

00:17:06.410 --> 00:17:09.130
This was leadership in the truest form.

00:17:09.130 --> 00:17:11.780
It wasn't easy and it wasn't popular.

00:17:11.780 --> 00:17:17.245
Your focus on return adjusted innovation
earned you nicknames, like Ruthless.

00:17:17.245 --> 00:17:22.097
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I was called Ruthful last week,

00:17:22.097 --> 00:17:23.040
it was not bad.

00:17:23.040 --> 00:17:24.575
I'm like, progress.

00:17:24.575 --> 00:17:25.820
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's very creative of them.

00:17:25.820 --> 00:17:28.510
And this was from people who were

00:17:28.510 --> 00:17:31.200
accustomed to a more
free wheeling culture.

00:17:31.200 --> 00:17:35.590
How did you balance the tradeoff
of instilling discipline

00:17:35.590 --> 00:17:38.980
with the appearance of curtailing
Google's innovative side?

00:17:40.360 --> 00:17:43.430
&gt;&gt; Well, first,
I think if I did not believe in my core,

00:17:43.430 --> 00:17:47.400
which I do, that investing for the
long-term is what every leader should do,

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:52.220
I wouldn't survive there and
I wouldn't be accepted.

00:17:52.220 --> 00:17:55.550
But I think that, what I've seen
throughout my career, is that if you don't

00:17:55.550 --> 00:17:59.130
invest for the longterm, you're actually
sowing the seeds of your own destruction.

00:17:59.130 --> 00:18:03.950
And I actually learned that really
early on at Morgan Stanley.

00:18:03.950 --> 00:18:10.740
I had the opportunity to work on an MNA
deal, the hostile defense of Gillette.

00:18:10.740 --> 00:18:15.080
And that deal was subsequently written
up in the book From Good to Great.

00:18:15.080 --> 00:18:19.480
And the reason was Gillette was
the subject of this hostile takeover,

00:18:19.480 --> 00:18:22.300
we were asked to find
a way to defend them.

00:18:22.300 --> 00:18:25.210
But the CEO at the time Colman Mockler,
I'll never forget it,

00:18:25.210 --> 00:18:27.210
the most extraordinary man.

00:18:27.210 --> 00:18:31.070
Said you can do anything you want but
you can't touch my R&amp;D budget.

00:18:31.070 --> 00:18:34.580
And I will not tell you what it's for
but I will tell you

00:18:34.580 --> 00:18:38.950
that I do not want to be independent
if I do not have those R&amp;D dollars.

00:18:38.950 --> 00:18:41.890
And at the time it was frustrating,
we're like we need some of it.

00:18:41.890 --> 00:18:43.090
We've got to have some of it.

00:18:43.090 --> 00:18:44.876
And he was adamant.

00:18:44.876 --> 00:18:47.696
We were able to keep the company
independent and years later,

00:18:47.696 --> 00:18:51.100
only years later,
did we learn that was the censor raiser.

00:18:51.100 --> 00:18:55.460
And he was right when he said, without
that R&amp;D budget, without planning for

00:18:55.460 --> 00:18:57.510
the future, it's just not worth it.

00:18:57.510 --> 00:19:01.880
So the most important point is,
I do believe it's imperative to invest for

00:19:01.880 --> 00:19:02.980
the long term.

00:19:02.980 --> 00:19:07.260
I think the other thing is
people need to make choices and

00:19:07.260 --> 00:19:09.560
that sort of goes to the financial crisis.

00:19:09.560 --> 00:19:11.930
I learned so
many things working with Hank Paulson.

00:19:11.930 --> 00:19:18.280
One of the things he said was that you
have to have the will and the means.

00:19:18.280 --> 00:19:20.430
And too often,
by the time you have the will,

00:19:20.430 --> 00:19:23.870
mainly the political will,
you no longer have the means.

00:19:23.870 --> 00:19:27.638
And when I moved down here from New York,
and I was often asked about the financial

00:19:27.638 --> 00:19:30.797
crisis, I thought that was a bit
odd because things are glorious and

00:19:30.797 --> 00:19:34.694
growing out here and that's very different
than the days in the financial crisis.

00:19:34.694 --> 00:19:39.549
But then I realized that some of those
lessons were as relevant here as they were

00:19:39.549 --> 00:19:44.261
during the financial crisis, which is
don't waste the really good time and

00:19:44.261 --> 00:19:48.457
spend without or invest without care
to whether it's a great idea or

00:19:48.457 --> 00:19:50.842
a mediocre idea just because you can.

00:19:50.842 --> 00:19:53.322
Put your effort behind
the things that matter.

00:19:53.322 --> 00:19:58.492
Make sure that you when you have
the means you also have the will and

00:19:58.492 --> 00:20:04.226
so all I was trying to do and have been
trying to do is create the visibility

00:20:04.226 --> 00:20:09.970
into the data so that our amazing
business partners can make the choice.

00:20:09.970 --> 00:20:11.320
It's not me making the choice.

00:20:11.320 --> 00:20:15.430
I think that would be grossly arrogant
to think that if you're not the product

00:20:15.430 --> 00:20:20.250
leader, you know how to stack-rank all of
the incredible things you're working on.

00:20:20.250 --> 00:20:23.330
But until people have the data,
it's hard to make the choices.

00:20:24.870 --> 00:20:29.730
There is a phrase also at an Alphabet
Google, anchor everything in data and

00:20:29.730 --> 00:20:31.150
the rest will follow.

00:20:31.150 --> 00:20:36.090
So my goal was actually to just
give them the data in a way

00:20:36.090 --> 00:20:40.570
that helped people better understand how
much they were putting behind each of

00:20:40.570 --> 00:20:44.270
the various options, so
they can then make choices in stack rank.

00:20:44.270 --> 00:20:47.770
Now, obviously, unless you squeeze
the envelope tight enough,

00:20:47.770 --> 00:20:49.130
nobody has to make choices.

00:20:49.130 --> 00:20:55.232
So, that's where probably the,
some of the nicknames come in.

00:20:55.232 --> 00:20:55.745
But-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:20:55.745 --> 00:20:56.785
&gt;&gt; But for all of you,

00:20:56.785 --> 00:20:59.615
the tools that you have,
the skills that you learned here,

00:20:59.615 --> 00:21:04.120
the ability to lay things out
clearly if you anchor everything in

00:21:04.120 --> 00:21:08.930
analytical rigor, the data should
help guide the best decision making.

00:21:10.090 --> 00:21:13.590
&gt;&gt; Was that something that you had
also done when you took on the CFO

00:21:13.590 --> 00:21:15.155
role at Morgan Stanley.

00:21:15.155 --> 00:21:18.760
Is that is not something that you had
to implement and place or was it there?

00:21:18.760 --> 00:21:23.360
&gt;&gt; I think that on every assignment
that I worked on as a banker.

00:21:23.360 --> 00:21:29.500
My view is, let's start with
a question to whoever the client is.

00:21:29.500 --> 00:21:34.200
Here's my understanding of
your hierarchy of objective.

00:21:34.200 --> 00:21:35.390
Do I have it right?

00:21:35.390 --> 00:21:39.230
Like, I want to hear what
people are solving for and

00:21:39.230 --> 00:21:43.000
it can be intellectual,
it can be emotional, whatever it is.

00:21:43.000 --> 00:21:45.230
What's your hierarchy of objectives?

00:21:45.230 --> 00:21:48.930
Once you know what you're solving for,
you can come up with the solution.

00:21:48.930 --> 00:21:52.350
And then the answer has to be able to be,
in my view,

00:21:52.350 --> 00:21:54.050
presented with analytical rigor.

00:21:54.050 --> 00:21:57.730
Because numbers are a way of framing
what's the opportunities set,

00:21:57.730 --> 00:22:00.730
what are the tradeoffs,
what's the downside potential.

00:22:00.730 --> 00:22:06.740
But the numbers actually force the clarity
of thought like one of my dad's lines.

00:22:06.740 --> 00:22:10.480
Again when I was a little kid he he
came home from slack and he said,

00:22:10.480 --> 00:22:15.450
if a physicist in his lab
could not define a quark

00:22:15.450 --> 00:22:19.670
in less than 60 seconds, they did not
know what they were talking about.

00:22:19.670 --> 00:22:22.970
And I've used that quark test for
years with colleagues.

00:22:22.970 --> 00:22:25.550
Like if you can't tell
me what you're doing and

00:22:25.550 --> 00:22:30.200
why in less than 60 seconds,
it's because you're not thinking clearly.

00:22:30.200 --> 00:22:33.970
And if you can't express it in numbers,
you're probably not thinking clearly.

00:22:33.970 --> 00:22:37.670
So that quark test at Morgan Stanley and
amongst my Google colleagues,

00:22:37.670 --> 00:22:39.070
they know what the quark test is.

00:22:39.070 --> 00:22:43.220
And it think it's a really helpful
way to try and get a pithy answer.

00:22:43.220 --> 00:22:49.151
Although this is not a pithy answer so you
may think that I've passed the quark test.

00:22:49.151 --> 00:22:51.707
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You passed.

00:22:51.707 --> 00:22:54.529
Okay, so turning to six months ago,

00:22:54.529 --> 00:22:59.940
thousands of employees walked out
of your offices around the world.

00:22:59.940 --> 00:23:04.710
After the New York Times reported
that Google paid over $90 million in

00:23:04.710 --> 00:23:08.290
exit packages to executive
accused of sexual misconduct.

00:23:10.010 --> 00:23:13.840
Your team walked out to and
you went with the them.

00:23:13.840 --> 00:23:14.340
Why?

00:23:15.980 --> 00:23:20.400
&gt;&gt; So I firmly believe that diversity and

00:23:20.400 --> 00:23:24.850
inclusion and
the highest of ethics is the way.

00:23:24.850 --> 00:23:29.932
We looked to comport ourselves and
we put so many programs and policies in

00:23:29.932 --> 00:23:35.449
place to ensure that we are continuing
to do things in the highest quality way.

00:23:35.449 --> 00:23:37.280
And we were all in this together.

00:23:37.280 --> 00:23:39.670
And to me that was
the most important method.

00:23:39.670 --> 00:23:42.290
We may not always get things right.

00:23:42.290 --> 00:23:46.350
But I think compared to every place
I've seen either its clients or

00:23:46.350 --> 00:23:50.150
wherever I think we try at
least as hard as anyone.

00:23:50.150 --> 00:23:54.327
And we are as a leadership team are really
committed to getting It right, and I just

00:23:54.327 --> 00:23:58.891
thought it was really important to be with
a team to say we're all in this together.

00:23:58.891 --> 00:24:04.510
We'll address issues, we'll solve issues,
we're all in this together.

00:24:04.510 --> 00:24:07.963
Thank you, it's an ongoing discussion, and

00:24:07.963 --> 00:24:13.019
that was very interesting to be able
to hear about it, so thank you.

00:24:14.410 --> 00:24:22.240
You did mention a comparison between
Wall Street and the financial industry.

00:24:22.240 --> 00:24:27.198
You were on Wall Street when it was being
skewered by occupiers, politicians,

00:24:27.198 --> 00:24:28.086
Main Street.

00:24:28.086 --> 00:24:31.206
And arguably now you're in Silicon Valley,

00:24:31.206 --> 00:24:36.553
when the canon is turning towards tech
giants, amid calls for data privacy.

00:24:36.553 --> 00:24:42.982
What learnings from a Wall Street context,
do you apply to Silicon Valley today?

00:24:42.982 --> 00:24:47.284
&gt;&gt; I think probably the most importance,
well, there are many important lessons.

00:24:47.284 --> 00:24:51.069
But one of the very important ones,
is to constantly try and

00:24:51.069 --> 00:24:52.818
raise the bar on yourself.

00:24:52.818 --> 00:24:58.952
And for us one of the really important
guiding kind of mantras internally.

00:24:58.952 --> 00:25:03.890
Is the importance to respect the user and
respect the opportunity.

00:25:03.890 --> 00:25:08.742
And we do keep trying to raise the bar
on ourselves on anything that will

00:25:08.742 --> 00:25:13.691
address both, either, of those
very important guiding principles.

00:25:13.691 --> 00:25:16.400
And you bring up privacy,
privacy is a great example.

00:25:16.400 --> 00:25:21.131
From the earliest days at Google,
there was a deeply felt feeling,

00:25:21.131 --> 00:25:24.456
deep recognition that
privacy is sacrosanct.

00:25:24.456 --> 00:25:25.995
That the data are your data, and

00:25:25.995 --> 00:25:29.080
that we need to make that really
clear in everything we do.

00:25:29.080 --> 00:25:34.050
So Google was the first to make sure
that you could take your data with you.

00:25:34.050 --> 00:25:38.490
All of the controls around privacy,
we do constantly research to try and

00:25:38.490 --> 00:25:40.770
make them as simple and
streamlined as possible.

00:25:40.770 --> 00:25:45.160
And just announced changes
to that again recently, and

00:25:45.160 --> 00:25:47.840
you know Cinder has been outspoken.

00:25:47.840 --> 00:25:52.396
Talking about the imperative to
support Federal legislation around

00:25:52.396 --> 00:25:53.578
privacy as well.

00:25:53.578 --> 00:25:59.759
We did 18 months of work leading up to
the European Privacy legislation, GDPR.

00:25:59.759 --> 00:26:03.222
So there are places where it's about
upping the bar on yourself, and

00:26:03.222 --> 00:26:05.890
speaking up where it makes sense for
legislation.

00:26:05.890 --> 00:26:10.714
In some places it is important to have
legislation that's a great example.

00:26:10.714 --> 00:26:13.636
In other places there are things
that companies should and

00:26:13.636 --> 00:26:15.215
could be doing individually.

00:26:15.215 --> 00:26:18.050
But I think I saw that
on Wall Street as well,

00:26:18.050 --> 00:26:21.690
some companies just kind of head in
the sand, I wish it were yesterday.

00:26:21.690 --> 00:26:23.045
And didn't recognize,

00:26:23.045 --> 00:26:27.660
no, there was a lot that needed to change,
some of which should be self-imposed.

00:26:27.660 --> 00:26:31.257
And could be more rapidly implemented
if you were doing it on your own.

00:26:31.257 --> 00:26:33.819
And somewhere actually
legislation did make sense.

00:26:38.543 --> 00:26:42.489
Okay, so legislation is interesting to me,
I have a question for

00:26:42.489 --> 00:26:44.081
you that is unique to you.

00:26:44.081 --> 00:26:48.606
You're one of the few here in
Silicon Valley who has also worked at

00:26:48.606 --> 00:26:50.592
the Department of Justice.

00:26:50.592 --> 00:26:51.658
&gt;&gt; And Treasury.

00:26:51.658 --> 00:26:53.890
&gt;&gt; And Treasury, yeah, well,
you were seconded to Treasury [LAUGH]

00:26:53.890 --> 00:26:57.132
&gt;&gt; Yes, Justice was right after Stanford.

00:26:57.132 --> 00:26:58.025
&gt;&gt; Yes, thank you.

00:26:58.025 --> 00:26:59.905
&gt;&gt; Wow, you did a lot of research.

00:26:59.905 --> 00:27:02.142
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You're my hero, Ruth,

00:27:02.142 --> 00:27:03.830
I've gotta research you.

00:27:03.830 --> 00:27:06.666
I may have known some of this organically.

00:27:06.666 --> 00:27:11.357
They, the Department of Justice, and
other policy makers around the world,

00:27:11.357 --> 00:27:12.645
are debating today.

00:27:12.645 --> 00:27:17.950
Whether technology companies like Google,
Amazon, and Facebook, are monopolies.

00:27:19.510 --> 00:27:24.158
As someone who's seen both sides,
do you think there's a point at

00:27:24.158 --> 00:27:27.905
which these big tech companies
might become too big?

00:27:27.905 --> 00:27:28.899
&gt;&gt; So a quick aside,

00:27:28.899 --> 00:27:32.545
I was a first year out of Stanford
intern in the Civil Division, so

00:27:32.545 --> 00:27:36.347
my answer has nothing to do with-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:27:36.347 --> 00:27:37.913
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] Justice Department was

00:27:37.913 --> 00:27:39.592
a great experience after Stanford.

00:27:39.592 --> 00:27:42.927
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That being said, to your question,

00:27:42.927 --> 00:27:45.804
I think that there's
a lot in the question.

00:27:45.804 --> 00:27:50.333
The reality is when you look at
the experience for consumers.

00:27:50.333 --> 00:27:55.652
We're all benefitting from ever-lower
prices, more access to more products.

00:27:55.652 --> 00:28:00.023
We're seeing innovation at a pace
that is extraordinary, and

00:28:00.023 --> 00:28:04.647
the funding around that last year,
was at the highest level ever.

00:28:04.647 --> 00:28:09.645
So there continues to be a really
vibrant ecosystem which I

00:28:09.645 --> 00:28:13.625
believe we're all
benefiting from as users.

00:28:13.625 --> 00:28:19.540
&gt;&gt; Great, you talked about
greatest source of vulnerability.

00:28:19.540 --> 00:28:23.334
What do you think is Google's
greatest source of vulnerability?

00:28:23.334 --> 00:28:24.736
&gt;&gt; So where that comes from,

00:28:24.736 --> 00:28:28.061
is one of the many lessons coming
out of the financial crisis.

00:28:28.061 --> 00:28:32.440
Was that it's imperative to identify your
greatest source of vulnerability, and

00:28:32.440 --> 00:28:34.930
protect against it in the good days.

00:28:34.930 --> 00:28:38.522
And for banks, it was liquidity,
but without liquidity you choke.

00:28:38.522 --> 00:28:40.664
And that's what happened
to individual banks and

00:28:40.664 --> 00:28:42.761
that's what happened to
the financial system.

00:28:42.761 --> 00:28:45.915
And so then I asked myself that question
when I got out here, I was kind of.

00:28:45.915 --> 00:28:49.095
If I'm going to talk about
that was one of the lessons,

00:28:49.095 --> 00:28:51.202
how do you apply it to technology?

00:28:51.202 --> 00:28:53.994
I think the greatest source
of vulnerability for

00:28:53.994 --> 00:28:58.950
technology is not continuing to invest for
the long-term and focus on innovation.

00:28:58.950 --> 00:29:02.714
So if you look at the companies in
technology that were the leaders 20 years,

00:29:02.714 --> 00:29:04.776
ago most of them are not
still the leaders.

00:29:04.776 --> 00:29:09.460
And so it really is this imperative
around investing for the long-term.

00:29:09.460 --> 00:29:13.310
And one of the many things I love
about the approach at Alphabet,

00:29:13.310 --> 00:29:14.818
from the earliest days.

00:29:14.818 --> 00:29:20.230
Was really trying to think through and
seed innovative new technologies.

00:29:20.230 --> 00:29:24.000
So in the earliest days,
they came up with an idea of 20% time.

00:29:24.000 --> 00:29:28.168
If you had a great idea, let's give
you 20% time to go pursue it, and

00:29:28.168 --> 00:29:30.687
out of that, for example, came Gmail,.

00:29:30.687 --> 00:29:32.136
Then it was the idea of X,

00:29:32.136 --> 00:29:35.730
what is now affectionately
known as our Moonshot Factory.

00:29:35.730 --> 00:29:38.960
Maybe you go do it 100% of the time,
and you try and come up with,

00:29:38.960 --> 00:29:40.106
what is that moonshot?

00:29:40.106 --> 00:29:44.307
That's where Waymo, which all of you
hopefully will be in soon enough.

00:29:44.307 --> 00:29:49.160
But that's where Waymo and our life
sciences business, Verily, came from.

00:29:49.160 --> 00:29:52.193
And Alphabet in many respects
was the third iteration,

00:29:52.193 --> 00:29:54.202
we tried to come up with a structure.

00:29:54.202 --> 00:29:57.332
We came up with a structure that said,
Google, focus over here.

00:29:57.332 --> 00:30:01.540
And let us really continue to go deeper
into what might be those next things that

00:30:01.540 --> 00:30:02.835
are out on the horizon.

00:30:02.835 --> 00:30:07.246
But I really think it is about the
imperative of focusing on the long-term

00:30:07.246 --> 00:30:10.060
and not getting pulled into short-termism.

00:30:10.060 --> 00:30:11.398
&gt;&gt; How does Alphabet,

00:30:11.398 --> 00:30:14.370
when you restructured it in 2014-
&gt;&gt; 15.

00:30:14.370 --> 00:30:18.834
&gt;&gt; 15, thank you, how does Alphabet
when you restructured in 2015.

00:30:18.834 --> 00:30:22.940
The vision you had for alphabet then,
how does that compare to what it is today?

00:30:24.150 --> 00:30:27.460
&gt;&gt; So time doesn't stop, but
I think that the main, the core of it,

00:30:27.460 --> 00:30:30.350
and Larry said it well in his letter.

00:30:30.350 --> 00:30:33.029
Announcing what are the goals for
Alphabet,

00:30:33.029 --> 00:30:35.576
was really to be able
to have Google focus.

00:30:35.576 --> 00:30:43.003
On the many extraordinary challenges and
opportunities that it faces.

00:30:43.003 --> 00:30:48.622
And not focus on kind of the next set of
things that are being done in other bets.

00:30:48.622 --> 00:30:53.492
And enable Larry and
Sergey to go really deep there,

00:30:53.492 --> 00:30:57.290
and so that's what we've been living.

00:30:57.290 --> 00:31:00.065
&gt;&gt; I have another
intersectional question for

00:31:00.065 --> 00:31:04.340
you because you have this old hat
of being a star investment banker.

00:31:04.340 --> 00:31:12.426
And now you have this recent hat of
leading this amazing company as the CFO.

00:31:12.426 --> 00:31:17.091
What do you think of the Uber IPO?

00:31:17.091 --> 00:31:21.951
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Uber is

00:31:21.951 --> 00:31:27.377
an extraordinary product,
I think it's changed all of our lives,

00:31:27.377 --> 00:31:32.449
and it's still very early,
it's early days post-pricing.

00:31:32.449 --> 00:31:37.260
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Okay, now turning to you as a leader,

00:31:37.260 --> 00:31:41.880
thank you, Ruth has seen 750 tech IPOs,
is that right?

00:31:41.880 --> 00:31:44.304
&gt;&gt; I haven't counted them all,
but it's been a lot.

00:31:44.304 --> 00:31:48.140
&gt;&gt; I've been counting, so-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:31:48.140 --> 00:31:50.260
[LAUGH]

00:31:50.260 --> 00:31:53.840
So you personally spent almost 30 years on

00:31:53.840 --> 00:31:59.870
Wall Street, where you grew businesses and
stabilized ones on the brink of crisis.

00:31:59.870 --> 00:32:02.490
And you're now here in
Silicon Valley where, as you said,

00:32:02.490 --> 00:32:04.760
you're positioning Alphabet for
long term success.

00:32:05.920 --> 00:32:10.430
What traits do you have that enable
you to be successful in two very

00:32:10.430 --> 00:32:11.831
different worlds?

00:32:15.517 --> 00:32:17.727
&gt;&gt; Let me sort of reframe that question,

00:32:17.727 --> 00:32:20.488
because I feel like 30
sounds like a long time, but

00:32:20.488 --> 00:32:25.350
I still feel like I've got a long way to
go, so the book fully hasn't been written.

00:32:25.350 --> 00:32:29.980
But what do I think is important if I
were turning back the clock to when I was

00:32:29.980 --> 00:32:31.265
exiting Wharton?

00:32:33.160 --> 00:32:36.850
I think one of the most important
things is clarity of thought, and

00:32:36.850 --> 00:32:45.910
really being able to analyze crisply and
articulate what you think is important.

00:32:45.910 --> 00:32:47.460
And I've already said it, but

00:32:47.460 --> 00:32:49.940
it's important to use your
voice at the right time.

00:32:49.940 --> 00:32:52.420
Don't be in a room and not use your voice.

00:32:52.420 --> 00:32:57.390
I think for the underrepresented
groups here that can be hard.

00:32:57.390 --> 00:33:00.540
And I think it's much better today
than it was when I got out, but

00:33:00.540 --> 00:33:05.840
the number of people who would speak
over me or sort of try and diminish you.

00:33:05.840 --> 00:33:09.620
And I even say today, this may seem
like a really odd place to go, but

00:33:09.620 --> 00:33:12.250
I think it's a really important
message for everyone.

00:33:12.250 --> 00:33:13.962
Which is when people speak over you,

00:33:13.962 --> 00:33:17.700
even though that may feel like it's
a small thing, it is not a small thing.

00:33:17.700 --> 00:33:20.120
Because every time you're in a meeting,

00:33:20.120 --> 00:33:23.620
if somebody is saying I don't really need
your voice, I'm going to speak over you.

00:33:23.620 --> 00:33:27.225
After a while, you sort of internalize and
say if you don't need my voice,

00:33:27.225 --> 00:33:28.800
why do you need me?

00:33:28.800 --> 00:33:32.590
And so it is really important
to stake out your place and

00:33:32.590 --> 00:33:33.980
make sure your voice is heard.

00:33:33.980 --> 00:33:35.540
And today it's easier to do that.

00:33:35.540 --> 00:33:38.570
If someone tries to speak over me,
I'll literally put my hand out.

00:33:38.570 --> 00:33:40.550
I'll say let me finish.

00:33:40.550 --> 00:33:43.390
Do that for others if you see it happen.

00:33:43.390 --> 00:33:46.089
And make sure you're working for
somebody who gives you that space.

00:33:47.120 --> 00:33:52.150
So finding your voice is really important,
and then getting a sponsor,

00:33:52.150 --> 00:33:57.280
finding someone who will take a risk
on you and open doors for you.

00:33:57.280 --> 00:34:02.040
Throughout my career, I could talk
about people who did that for me, but

00:34:02.040 --> 00:34:07.750
maybe one of the most important, other
than Bill Campbell, was the conversation

00:34:07.750 --> 00:34:12.380
I had when I was asked to take over
running the financial institutions group.

00:34:12.380 --> 00:34:15.570
And I'd had a career where I
covered technology companies,

00:34:15.570 --> 00:34:21.320
and media companies, and private equity
companies, really fun stuff, in my view.

00:34:21.320 --> 00:34:25.580
And then I was asked to cover banks, and
asset managers, and insurance companies.

00:34:25.580 --> 00:34:28.410
And so probably not surprising to
some of you my reaction was wow,

00:34:28.410 --> 00:34:31.780
that sounds really boring,
why would I do that?

00:34:31.780 --> 00:34:34.632
And the person who went on to become
the president of Morgan Stanley said,

00:34:34.632 --> 00:34:35.715
you gotta trust me on this.

00:34:35.715 --> 00:34:39.370
And he literally said it's going to open
doors that you're not even aware of,

00:34:39.370 --> 00:34:41.110
I can't even tell you what they are.

00:34:41.110 --> 00:34:42.930
This was in 2006.

00:34:42.930 --> 00:34:45.550
My instinct yet again was just wrong.

00:34:45.550 --> 00:34:48.098
It was no, that sounds boring,
I don't want to do it.

00:34:48.098 --> 00:34:53.665
But when he said trust me on this, I said
okay, and then August of 2007, when you

00:34:53.665 --> 00:34:58.420
were actually at a bank is when you first
saw the cracks in the financial system.

00:34:58.420 --> 00:35:03.200
And so I had not quite a year
to figure out which way was up

00:35:03.200 --> 00:35:06.130
when it was clear there were
problems on the horizon.

00:35:06.130 --> 00:35:08.110
That led to the role with Treasury and

00:35:08.110 --> 00:35:13.300
Fed, that led to the CFO role at Morgan
Stanley, that led to being out here.

00:35:13.300 --> 00:35:18.283
So I think sponsors and having that
honest conversation are really important.

00:35:18.283 --> 00:35:21.517
So I'd sort of put all of that together.

00:35:21.517 --> 00:35:26.070
&gt;&gt; I understand from a conversation
I had with Jeff Immelt last week,

00:35:26.070 --> 00:35:30.785
a former partner of yours,
that you not only have a strong voice, but

00:35:30.785 --> 00:35:35.282
he said that you're the only person
he likes to get bad news from.

00:35:35.282 --> 00:35:37.400
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I gave him a lot of bad news.

00:35:37.400 --> 00:35:41.920
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] And to that end he
said that you have incredible

00:35:41.920 --> 00:35:45.990
serene sense of calm that was incredibly
rare during the financial crisis,

00:35:45.990 --> 00:35:50.410
and you were the only one he saw
through the whole storm that had it.

00:35:53.600 --> 00:35:55.075
&gt;&gt; That's just a comment.

00:35:55.075 --> 00:35:58.267
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; No, I wanted to toot your horn for you,

00:35:58.267 --> 00:35:59.881
because I know you may not.

00:35:59.881 --> 00:36:04.507
&gt;&gt; Actually during that period, what was
fascinating to me about him is he kept

00:36:04.507 --> 00:36:08.570
wanting to get the bad news, and
not that he wanted to get bad news.

00:36:08.570 --> 00:36:10.750
He wanted to get what I thought was truth,
let me put it that way.

00:36:10.750 --> 00:36:12.560
I think he was hoping some
of it would not be bad.

00:36:12.560 --> 00:36:17.535
But I also think that it's really
important on your team to make sure that

00:36:17.535 --> 00:36:22.759
you are hearing those voices that are
telling you something you don't want to

00:36:22.759 --> 00:36:28.240
hear, and creating a space where you're
drawing all of that information out.

00:36:28.240 --> 00:36:32.399
And in the book, Trillion Dollar Coach,
one of the comments Bill Campbell makes

00:36:32.399 --> 00:36:35.150
is always make sure,
before you opine as the leader,

00:36:35.150 --> 00:36:38.966
that you've made sure you've heard
every voice that has not yet spoken.

00:36:38.966 --> 00:36:43.164
Because often times some of the ones who
are most quiet might actually have the new

00:36:43.164 --> 00:36:45.000
idea, the most profound idea.

00:36:45.000 --> 00:36:50.530
And Jeff always wanted to hear
whatever I was concerned about,

00:36:50.530 --> 00:36:51.480
whatever the thought was.

00:36:51.480 --> 00:36:55.000
It was the challenge to him to the way
he was trying to think about it.

00:36:55.000 --> 00:36:57.330
And I think that's an important lesson for
us.

00:36:57.330 --> 00:36:59.270
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:36:59.270 --> 00:37:04.405
You may know that role playing is a time
honored tradition here at the GSB.

00:37:04.405 --> 00:37:05.267
&gt;&gt; Uh-oh.

00:37:05.267 --> 00:37:07.860
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We love to do it.

00:37:07.860 --> 00:37:08.890
Might you indulge me?

00:37:08.890 --> 00:37:11.258
&gt;&gt; Yes, please, go, get some water.

00:37:11.258 --> 00:37:18.160
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So Donald Trump calls.

00:37:18.160 --> 00:37:24.110
He needs a leader who knows markets and
technology, no one better than you.

00:37:24.110 --> 00:37:28.690
He says, Ruth, I need you to come
on as my Treasury Secretary.

00:37:28.690 --> 00:37:29.190
What do you say?

00:37:31.260 --> 00:37:35.443
&gt;&gt; Well, that call hasn't come, and-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:37:35.443 --> 00:37:38.546
&gt;&gt; I am really focused on what I am doing

00:37:38.546 --> 00:37:42.020
right now, so,
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:37:42.020 --> 00:37:43.770
&gt;&gt; Thank you for humoring me.

00:37:43.770 --> 00:37:48.667
Let me now turn it over to my friends
in the audience to ask you their

00:37:48.667 --> 00:37:49.923
own questions.

00:37:52.321 --> 00:37:53.357
Right there?

00:37:53.357 --> 00:37:58.188
[BLANK AUDIO]
&gt;&gt; Hi,

00:37:58.188 --> 00:37:59.780
Ruth, thank you for being here today.

00:37:59.780 --> 00:38:02.860
You spoke a lot about
having a good sponsor.

00:38:02.860 --> 00:38:06.429
What were the things that you looked for
in a sponsor that you identified?

00:38:10.385 --> 00:38:13.985
&gt;&gt; Someone who is smart and
extremely ethical, so

00:38:13.985 --> 00:38:19.850
when they were solving a problem they
were thinking about what I also needed.

00:38:19.850 --> 00:38:24.120
It wasn't just being expedient in
solving one of their problems,

00:38:24.120 --> 00:38:25.230
somebody I could learn from.

00:38:26.690 --> 00:38:31.115
Early in my career at Morgan Stanley I
worked on a deal with somebody who

00:38:31.115 --> 00:38:33.965
never took me even to internal meetings,
and

00:38:33.965 --> 00:38:38.257
after a pretty long period of time
he took me to an internal meeting.

00:38:38.257 --> 00:38:42.157
And this senior person on the deal team,
the partner, turned to him and

00:38:42.157 --> 00:38:45.226
pointed at me and
said is this the I I keep hearing about?

00:38:45.226 --> 00:38:48.215
And made it really clear that he had,
for months, been taking credit for

00:38:48.215 --> 00:38:48.859
all of my work.

00:38:49.920 --> 00:38:52.970
And I said, you know what,
that's got to stop right now.

00:38:52.970 --> 00:38:56.270
And so I thought of one of
the smartest people in the department.

00:38:56.270 --> 00:38:59.600
He had a reputation for
being really difficult, but

00:38:59.600 --> 00:39:01.370
I knew I could learn from him.

00:39:01.370 --> 00:39:03.750
And I went to him and said,
I want to work on anything you have,

00:39:03.750 --> 00:39:05.850
whatever it might be,
I just want to learn from you.

00:39:05.850 --> 00:39:07.650
I've heard you're amazing.

00:39:07.650 --> 00:39:10.504
People like complements,
and so he said okay.

00:39:10.504 --> 00:39:13.760
[LAUGH] So
he ended up putting me on a deal, and

00:39:13.760 --> 00:39:15.830
the first deal was a pretty curl me deal.

00:39:15.830 --> 00:39:19.250
But I learned a ton from him,

00:39:19.250 --> 00:39:22.000
he actually is the person who
got me on that Gillette deal.

00:39:22.000 --> 00:39:23.290
He then opened the door and

00:39:23.290 --> 00:39:26.000
working on something that was
mark key opened another door.

00:39:26.000 --> 00:39:30.190
That was the first time I had
somebody who I think actually said,

00:39:30.190 --> 00:39:31.640
let me give it a go.

00:39:31.640 --> 00:39:33.850
So you have to prove
yourself to the person.

00:39:33.850 --> 00:39:37.390
Oftentimes, people will come up to me and
say, will you be my sponsor?

00:39:37.390 --> 00:39:40.990
And to me that's almost akin to saying,
can we have a child, on the first date.

00:39:40.990 --> 00:39:42.340
No, you can't.

00:39:42.340 --> 00:39:43.390
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; First, let's have a date,

00:39:43.390 --> 00:39:45.000
let's prove I want to have a second date,

00:39:45.000 --> 00:39:47.940
then maybe further down the line
we can talk about that.

00:39:47.940 --> 00:39:50.300
So you do have to earn the right.

00:39:50.300 --> 00:39:52.608
To have that kind of a relationship.

00:39:52.608 --> 00:39:54.520
It's a two-way relationship.

00:39:54.520 --> 00:39:57.290
The other thing I learned
early in my career, I was so

00:39:57.290 --> 00:40:00.350
grateful to somebody who was a sponsor,
so grateful for

00:40:00.350 --> 00:40:03.450
all the doors he was opening, and
then I realized, you know what?

00:40:03.450 --> 00:40:06.260
He's getting at least as much
out of this as I am, because

00:40:06.260 --> 00:40:09.490
I'm great operating leverage for him,
because he's getting these stretch goals.

00:40:09.490 --> 00:40:10.910
So I'm super excited.

00:40:10.910 --> 00:40:11.900
I'm learning.

00:40:11.900 --> 00:40:16.590
It's amazing to be in a position where
you're feeling like you're growing.

00:40:16.590 --> 00:40:19.700
So it's really a two-way street and
recognizing that as well,

00:40:19.700 --> 00:40:23.760
but to your question, you earn it and
make sure the person's really smart,

00:40:23.760 --> 00:40:25.290
respected, and super ethical.

00:40:35.680 --> 00:40:39.417
&gt;&gt; Hi, Ruth,
you have a lot of very unique experiences,

00:40:39.417 --> 00:40:45.210
not the least of which having been worked
both in the private sector, the Treasury.

00:40:45.210 --> 00:40:49.560
And during the financial crisis,
presumably you also interacted a lot with

00:40:49.560 --> 00:40:52.820
academics, who were also advising
the government at the time.

00:40:52.820 --> 00:40:58.120
How would you compare and contrast the
different ways in which the government,

00:40:58.120 --> 00:41:03.050
the private sector, and the academic
institutions lead and shape our world?

00:41:04.050 --> 00:41:06.190
&gt;&gt; It's a great question.

00:41:06.190 --> 00:41:10.660
For me one of the most important
things is to to find a place where you

00:41:10.660 --> 00:41:13.440
care about the mission of
the organization you're part of.

00:41:13.440 --> 00:41:15.720
Whether it's in a public sector or
the private sector,

00:41:15.720 --> 00:41:20.270
being mission driven is
a critical element of it.

00:41:21.940 --> 00:41:26.910
What was intriguing when you're
working with the public sector

00:41:26.910 --> 00:41:29.666
is that often times it's tough,

00:41:29.666 --> 00:41:35.410
you have different constituents who
are judging what are the outcomes.

00:41:35.410 --> 00:41:38.330
And you need to balance sort of

00:41:38.330 --> 00:41:42.160
perception elements as much as
the content elements of it.

00:41:42.160 --> 00:41:43.950
How's it going to land because you have so

00:41:43.950 --> 00:41:46.330
many public constituents that
are looking at what you're doing?

00:41:46.330 --> 00:41:50.220
But I think in each instance,
if I go back to the work at Treasury,

00:41:50.220 --> 00:41:51.700
what were the key elements?

00:41:51.700 --> 00:41:56.627
It goes back to exactly what I was
doing with private sector clients.

00:41:56.627 --> 00:42:00.780
It's what are the analytics
around any judgement?

00:42:00.780 --> 00:42:02.320
How much capital is needed in something?

00:42:02.320 --> 00:42:03.750
Well, you need to put data around it.

00:42:03.750 --> 00:42:08.790
We ended up, as an example, running
mortgage analytics to figure out how

00:42:08.790 --> 00:42:13.310
big the hole could be 24 hours a day
in three locations around the globe,

00:42:13.310 --> 00:42:16.780
because we didn't have enough
time to just do it normal way.

00:42:16.780 --> 00:42:20.200
And yet it was the analytics
that were important, so

00:42:20.200 --> 00:42:24.920
I really feel like in any setting,
what are you solving for?

00:42:24.920 --> 00:42:28.360
What's the data that's going to
help you make the best decision?

00:42:28.360 --> 00:42:32.340
And who can you pull in for
the best advice and this instinct based on

00:42:32.340 --> 00:42:36.960
experience, whether it's academics, public
market, private market, is what's needed.

00:42:36.960 --> 00:42:41.410
Hank Paulson on these weekends we were
down there, he literally would pop in and

00:42:41.410 --> 00:42:44.440
out of our conference room with ideas and
those ideas,

00:42:44.440 --> 00:42:47.010
it was because he had
instinct based on experience.

00:42:47.010 --> 00:42:51.720
And we were just looking for
whatever the least worst solution was.

00:42:51.720 --> 00:42:56.500
And so it was this mix of public-private
that actually helped inform

00:42:56.500 --> 00:42:57.470
how do we move forward?

00:42:57.470 --> 00:43:02.450
So I'm not sure if I've fully answered it,
but I don't view them as distinct,

00:43:02.450 --> 00:43:05.120
because it's problem solving for
something that's important.

00:43:09.890 --> 00:43:15.030
&gt;&gt; Hi, Ruth, I had a question around the
large bets that Google continues to make.

00:43:15.030 --> 00:43:19.870
So Google consistently has obviously
played a role buying large companies,

00:43:19.870 --> 00:43:21.520
YouTube, Android.

00:43:21.520 --> 00:43:25.460
And over the past few years
maybe has invested less so

00:43:25.460 --> 00:43:27.130
in some of these transformational bets.

00:43:28.320 --> 00:43:31.960
I would be curious to know,
as Google continues to grow and

00:43:31.960 --> 00:43:34.730
the core business continues to
be something to think about,

00:43:34.730 --> 00:43:38.770
do you think transformational bets
are important whether acquisitions,

00:43:38.770 --> 00:43:42.312
strategic investments or another strategy?

00:43:42.312 --> 00:43:47.220
&gt;&gt; I absolutely think as I said
a couple of times now investing for

00:43:47.220 --> 00:43:50.450
the long term is imperative and
it needs to be.

00:43:50.450 --> 00:43:54.440
It needs it to be at a scale that matters,
that can make a difference,

00:43:54.440 --> 00:44:01.360
that can really improve lives and
so, you framed the question well,

00:44:01.360 --> 00:44:06.390
which is that's both organic and
acquisition and I think both are fruitful.

00:44:06.390 --> 00:44:11.850
We're investing quite aggressively in
a number of areas that we do view as

00:44:11.850 --> 00:44:14.250
important long term opportunities for so

00:44:14.250 --> 00:44:18.480
one of the most exciting areas is what's
going on with the cloud right now.

00:44:18.480 --> 00:44:22.180
And we may have started later
than we should have, but

00:44:22.180 --> 00:44:25.985
we have the core engineering
pillars that are critical.

00:44:25.985 --> 00:44:29.060
It's the technical infrastructure,
the data analytics,

00:44:29.060 --> 00:44:33.350
it's security, it's collaborative tools,
it's the machine learning opportunity.

00:44:33.350 --> 00:44:38.360
We needed to bring it together, and we're
investing meaningfully because our view Is

00:44:38.360 --> 00:44:42.940
that we're very early globally
in this move to the cloud and

00:44:42.940 --> 00:44:44.900
it's an absolutely transformative one.

00:44:44.900 --> 00:44:49.750
It opens up new opportunities for
every business as they think about what

00:44:49.750 --> 00:44:53.780
are the efficiencies and
tools that they can benefit from and so

00:44:53.780 --> 00:44:56.130
that as an example is
a really important area.

00:44:56.130 --> 00:45:00.950
You look at the way we're
all using devices today and

00:45:00.950 --> 00:45:03.250
how do you think about what
are these home devices?

00:45:03.250 --> 00:45:08.666
It's desktop, it's mobile, it's also
what are the home devices that one has

00:45:08.666 --> 00:45:15.860
that enable you to get whatever you
want from, for us, the Google Home.

00:45:15.860 --> 00:45:16.800
And hopefully you all have it,

00:45:16.800 --> 00:45:21.070
listen to music, get recipes,
do whatever you want to do, phone calls.

00:45:21.070 --> 00:45:24.610
And so these are,
our hardware effort is important.

00:45:24.610 --> 00:45:28.050
What we're doing with YouTube and
subscriptions in YouTube TV is important.

00:45:28.050 --> 00:45:29.430
What we're doing with Weimo's important.

00:45:29.430 --> 00:45:32.910
We're doing a lot in health, which is
probably one of the most exciting areas

00:45:32.910 --> 00:45:35.240
when you think about
applying machine learning.

00:45:35.240 --> 00:45:37.400
So absolutely, when we look at it,

00:45:37.400 --> 00:45:41.280
we've got an extraordinary business
in what we're doing with search.

00:45:41.280 --> 00:45:44.810
We're continuing to build on that
through investments in machine learning.

00:45:44.810 --> 00:45:48.690
And then we're layering on some newer
areas and if you're going to continue to

00:45:48.690 --> 00:45:50.700
invest for the long term,
you need to keep seeding those.

00:45:50.700 --> 00:45:52.560
They don't need to all be acquisition.

00:45:52.560 --> 00:45:54.490
We do a lot of acquisitions, but

00:45:54.490 --> 00:45:58.440
you need to keep thinking about where the
world can go or where you want to take it.

00:46:00.800 --> 00:46:01.590
Okay.

00:46:01.590 --> 00:46:02.980
&gt;&gt; One last question please.

00:46:04.620 --> 00:46:09.140
&gt;&gt; Ruth, I used to work one of
those long bets at sidewalk labs.

00:46:09.140 --> 00:46:12.210
&gt;&gt; Can people hear you though?

00:46:12.210 --> 00:46:15.770
The oak hole at sidewalk labs, yes.

00:46:17.310 --> 00:46:18.465
The mic's coming.

00:46:18.465 --> 00:46:24.210
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Could

00:46:24.210 --> 00:46:27.200
you talk about being a sponsor
now that you're senior, and

00:46:27.200 --> 00:46:29.820
how you decide to allocate that time and
what that looks like for you?

00:46:31.000 --> 00:46:32.450
&gt;&gt; I'm sorry, can you go back to.

00:46:32.450 --> 00:46:37.160
&gt;&gt; Can you talk about how you're a sponsor
now that you are in a senior position, and

00:46:37.160 --> 00:46:39.360
how you allocate your time and
what that looks like for you?

00:46:40.910 --> 00:46:42.870
&gt;&gt; So I thought you were
going to ask a Sidewalk question.

00:46:42.870 --> 00:46:43.700
For those of you who don't know,

00:46:43.700 --> 00:46:48.420
Sidewalk is looking at reimagining what
is a city and how do we think about

00:46:48.420 --> 00:46:53.060
every element of it, from mobility
to building to affordable housing.

00:46:53.060 --> 00:46:56.950
And this question about where does this
go I think is a really exciting one for

00:46:56.950 --> 00:47:00.430
us because it ties into some of
the things we're doing with Weimo.

00:47:00.430 --> 00:47:03.790
What if you don't need to spend
the kind of money Stanford spends and

00:47:03.790 --> 00:47:05.340
others do on parking garages?

00:47:05.340 --> 00:47:08.850
And you can put that into schools and
education and playgrounds.

00:47:08.850 --> 00:47:10.500
I'm going to come to your question,
I promise.

00:47:10.500 --> 00:47:13.990
But when you said Sidewalk I'm like,
there's a lot going on in the world of

00:47:13.990 --> 00:47:18.620
reimagining what can life be, let alone
the number of lives that can be saved

00:47:18.620 --> 00:47:21.348
when you go to self-driving cars, but
you asked a different question, sponsors.

00:47:21.348 --> 00:47:24.130
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Look, I think that, to me,

00:47:24.130 --> 00:47:29.510
it's exciting when you look at people who
are trying to figure out where to next.

00:47:29.510 --> 00:47:33.490
As I've said many times,
I am so grateful and can name so

00:47:33.490 --> 00:47:39.440
many sponsors who've really been key to
my career, so my view is that I try and

00:47:39.440 --> 00:47:45.010
spend time every week and
in structured settings on in

00:47:45.010 --> 00:47:48.200
that individually or with groups because I
think there are a couple of ways that you

00:47:48.200 --> 00:47:52.210
can go about Actually helping people
as they're thinking about what next?

00:47:53.460 --> 00:47:56.600
And it's really about investing
in someone's career and

00:47:56.600 --> 00:47:58.920
helping them understand what
are their options to me.

00:47:58.920 --> 00:48:04.538
We owe it to our teams to understand
what the kind of the career can be.

00:48:04.538 --> 00:48:08.045
I'm literally going through with
my team now, so, and my question

00:48:08.045 --> 00:48:12.121
is on the scale of one to ten, where ten
is high, one is low, how happy are you?

00:48:12.121 --> 00:48:13.310
How long do you want to be here?

00:48:13.310 --> 00:48:14.300
What do you want next?

00:48:14.300 --> 00:48:15.710
What's important to you?

00:48:15.710 --> 00:48:19.440
And those conversations are really
valuable conversations.

00:48:19.440 --> 00:48:22.730
I like to set the tone and
drive those, and

00:48:22.730 --> 00:48:25.190
that to me is about sponsoring my team.

00:48:25.190 --> 00:48:28.699
But then, I'm also setting out
a note to all my team is, okay,

00:48:28.699 --> 00:48:29.972
I just finished that.

00:48:29.972 --> 00:48:33.304
If I have the time to do it, I want my
team to be doing it with their team.

00:48:33.304 --> 00:48:38.122
And so ensuring you're in a place where
those types of practices cascade is

00:48:38.122 --> 00:48:39.430
really important.

00:48:41.550 --> 00:48:44.770
&gt;&gt; Ruth, half of us here will
have graduated next month.

00:48:44.770 --> 00:48:46.210
&gt;&gt; Congratulations all of you,

00:48:46.210 --> 00:48:48.609
why don't we see the hands
from the about to graduate?

00:48:50.000 --> 00:48:50.700
Wow!

00:48:50.700 --> 00:48:51.850
Awesome, awesome!

00:48:51.850 --> 00:48:52.800
Very exciting.

00:48:52.800 --> 00:48:54.990
A lot of exciting things ahead of you.

00:48:54.990 --> 00:48:58.970
I will assure you that I had no idea
I would be in this role when I was

00:48:58.970 --> 00:49:02.035
the equivalent of your seat,
and life is really wonderful.

00:49:02.035 --> 00:49:05.404
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Case in point.

00:49:05.404 --> 00:49:11.309
You are our last hope for our final
view from the top wisdom [LAUGH].

00:49:11.309 --> 00:49:16.189
I thought I'd ask you some questions that
I know are keeping us all up at night.

00:49:16.189 --> 00:49:18.370
Are you ready for a lightning round?

00:49:18.370 --> 00:49:19.360
&gt;&gt; Sure, go for it.

00:49:19.360 --> 00:49:24.320
&gt;&gt; Okay, watch Game of Thrones or
eat ice cream at Salt and Straw?

00:49:24.320 --> 00:49:25.322
Man.

00:49:25.322 --> 00:49:28.400
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; What if I don't do either?

00:49:28.400 --> 00:49:31.989
My husband likes Game of Thrones,
my kids like Game of Thrones,

00:49:31.989 --> 00:49:34.918
it gives me a couple-hour
break to go do what I want.

00:49:34.918 --> 00:49:39.822
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Iceland is

00:49:39.822 --> 00:49:44.257
a fun trip if you want to go there,
so yeah, that's the truth.

00:49:44.257 --> 00:49:49.960
&gt;&gt; How about going to tech or finance?

00:49:49.960 --> 00:49:52.480
&gt;&gt; Well, I found my cake and
eat it too because I'm doing both.

00:49:52.480 --> 00:49:58.810
But look, I think that go,
I view this is a maybe

00:49:58.810 --> 00:50:03.780
a little old fashioned but I tell my kids
these stills I hope it's a good advice.

00:50:03.780 --> 00:50:07.140
I feel like the experiences
you're about to have

00:50:07.140 --> 00:50:09.680
are a paid post-graduate education.

00:50:09.680 --> 00:50:13.190
Don't view it as, well, as you said,
on average, it's five years and

00:50:13.190 --> 00:50:14.810
then you go do something else.

00:50:14.810 --> 00:50:18.540
Be at the place where you can learn a ton,
you're proud to be there, it has

00:50:18.540 --> 00:50:21.990
the ethics and culture you want, and
you're working with someone extraordinary.

00:50:21.990 --> 00:50:24.160
Who knows where that's going to take you?

00:50:24.160 --> 00:50:27.250
And if that is tech first or
finance first and

00:50:27.250 --> 00:50:30.930
it resonates with you, you are not
shutting doors, you are opening doors.

00:50:30.930 --> 00:50:34.900
And just like being here at
GSB opens more doors for you,

00:50:34.900 --> 00:50:36.930
just make sure that
that's what you're doing.

00:50:36.930 --> 00:50:40.190
because you're on a journey and
you're very, very early.

00:50:40.190 --> 00:50:42.770
And so I am firmly of
the view I wouldn't have had

00:50:42.770 --> 00:50:45.650
this opportunity had I
not done my prior one.

00:50:45.650 --> 00:50:46.890
And one thing led to another.

00:50:46.890 --> 00:50:48.460
So I'm not trying to skirt your answer but

00:50:48.460 --> 00:50:52.000
I really think it is more about the people
and quality of what you're doing

00:50:53.240 --> 00:50:55.790
rather than should it be tech or
health care or something else.

00:50:55.790 --> 00:51:00.020
Unless you have this unbelievable
commitment and dying passion for that.

00:51:00.020 --> 00:51:04.708
&gt;&gt; Should we pay off student debt or
invest in retirement savings.

00:51:04.708 --> 00:51:07.850
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Wow!

00:51:07.850 --> 00:51:09.360
I'd get rid of that student debt.

00:51:09.360 --> 00:51:10.773
All right.

00:51:10.773 --> 00:51:12.520
&gt;&gt; You can do the rest later.

00:51:12.520 --> 00:51:18.807
&gt;&gt; Should we have kids now or later?

00:51:18.807 --> 00:51:20.032
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:51:20.032 --> 00:51:24.380
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Should we join a company or

00:51:24.380 --> 00:51:25.180
found a company?

00:51:26.827 --> 00:51:31.670
&gt;&gt; Man, I would have been
a horrible founder of a company and

00:51:31.670 --> 00:51:36.900
I'm not so bad at a company but there
are others who are just the opposite.

00:51:36.900 --> 00:51:40.140
Again, don't regret anything.

00:51:40.140 --> 00:51:44.650
I remember early on reading Jeff Bases'
no regrets point, which is When

00:51:44.650 --> 00:51:49.530
he was looking to leave DE Shaw hedge fund
to create this crazy idealist book seller,

00:51:49.530 --> 00:51:52.640
I think his parents said to him,
what are you doing?

00:51:52.640 --> 00:51:55.720
I don't know if he paid off his student
loans at that point, but he said,

00:51:55.720 --> 00:51:56.750
I don't want to have regrets.

00:51:56.750 --> 00:51:59.466
I'd rather try and
fail than never try at all, so

00:51:59.466 --> 00:52:04.314
I think the regrets analysis is actually
really good, don't ever regret something.

00:52:04.314 --> 00:52:09.351
You've got plenty of time
&gt;&gt; Big Data or bit-sized data?

00:52:09.351 --> 00:52:11.201
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:52:11.201 --> 00:52:14.983
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:52:14.983 --> 00:52:17.391
&gt;&gt; I think there's a lot that's phenomenal

00:52:17.391 --> 00:52:22.150
that we're living in and we haven't talked
that much about AI machine learning.

00:52:22.150 --> 00:52:25.195
But we are living in an extraordinary,
am I supposed to have one word answers?

00:52:25.195 --> 00:52:26.844
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Please No, It's okay.

00:52:26.844 --> 00:52:30.542
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We're living in an extraordinary time.

00:52:30.542 --> 00:52:35.443
When I look at what AI machine learning
on building data can do it's not

00:52:35.443 --> 00:52:40.178
only the fun stuff, like if you were
using one of our Pixel phones and

00:52:40.178 --> 00:52:42.256
our photos, Google Photos,

00:52:42.256 --> 00:52:47.020
You can take pictures and sort by
person by hug by whatever you want.

00:52:47.020 --> 00:52:50.280
You can go to any country and
translated any language but,

00:52:50.280 --> 00:52:53.170
you also to me what's super
exciting is what's going on.

00:52:53.170 --> 00:52:55.411
I've already mentioned
it with health care.

00:52:55.411 --> 00:53:00.866
As an example, one of the most amazing
areas it's what's going on with scans and

00:53:00.866 --> 00:53:04.560
pathology reports, reading reports.

00:53:04.560 --> 00:53:09.812
In the area of breast cancer,
we just recently had this extraordinary

00:53:09.812 --> 00:53:14.798
breakthrough, where the accuracy
of diagnosing stages of breast

00:53:14.798 --> 00:53:19.887
cancer is meaningfully better with
the tools of machine learning.

00:53:19.887 --> 00:53:26.058
And as an example,
I think it's In one of four instances when

00:53:26.058 --> 00:53:33.450
doctors are reading pathology reports
over different periods of time.

00:53:33.450 --> 00:53:35.890
They change their
diagnosis of the staging.

00:53:35.890 --> 00:53:40.050
Staging is critical because it tells you
what kind of intervention is needed how

00:53:40.050 --> 00:53:47.160
far along you are, and what's also
extraordinary is the percentage of time

00:53:47.160 --> 00:53:52.810
they missed early stages of metastatic
cancer is actually quite remarkable.

00:53:52.810 --> 00:53:57.650
From our early research,
what we're seeing is in almost all cases,

00:53:57.650 --> 00:54:02.800
when you're looking at pathology reports,
biopsies, the machine

00:54:02.800 --> 00:54:07.920
learning is able to come up with what are
signs of early stage metastatic cancer.

00:54:07.920 --> 00:54:09.250
Why is this so exciting?

00:54:09.250 --> 00:54:13.590
Because it's building on data in
ways that is a tool for doctors.

00:54:13.590 --> 00:54:17.760
And what they're saying is this enables
them to focus on the tough stuff.

00:54:17.760 --> 00:54:19.250
It enables them to go deeper.

00:54:19.250 --> 00:54:22.940
It enables them to know when to
intervene in an early space,

00:54:22.940 --> 00:54:26.200
as a cancer survivor
myself I look at this and

00:54:26.200 --> 00:54:31.160
I say, it's sort of akin to letting
doctors have the tools they need to

00:54:31.160 --> 00:54:34.720
be as effective as possible and spend
time with patients the way they want.

00:54:34.720 --> 00:54:39.390
So I get excited about these things
because I look at the ability to transform

00:54:39.390 --> 00:54:40.480
care.

00:54:40.480 --> 00:54:44.990
And I look at what we have being at
Stanford or what I had being in New York.

00:54:44.990 --> 00:54:45.730
It's amazing,

00:54:45.730 --> 00:54:48.980
not everyone around the world lives
in places as extraordinary as this.

00:54:48.980 --> 00:54:55.050
And so, really, to provide that kind of
access to quality diagnosis, amazing.

00:54:55.050 --> 00:54:59.450
One more story, I was with a doctor
recently and I asked him about this.

00:54:59.450 --> 00:55:05.240
And he said, It's amazing, the ability to
see what's going on in my patient's body

00:55:05.240 --> 00:55:10.020
on a continuous basis rather than just the
15 minutes or 30 minutes they're with me.

00:55:10.020 --> 00:55:13.730
I am not much better, and so
I look at that and I say,

00:55:13.730 --> 00:55:16.440
we're living in an amazing time.

00:55:16.440 --> 00:55:19.070
And to be able to leverage
this the right way.

00:55:19.070 --> 00:55:23.010
To address whether it's health care or

00:55:25.470 --> 00:55:29.740
deaths on the roads from cars, one thing
after another, it's pretty extraordinary

00:55:29.740 --> 00:55:33.610
what we're able to do, and I think we're
going to look back on this and say wow.

00:55:33.610 --> 00:55:34.790
How much more can we apply?

00:55:36.310 --> 00:55:37.740
&gt;&gt; Rao, thank you so much.

00:55:37.740 --> 00:55:38.355
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:55:38.355 --> 00:55:40.814
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:55:40.814 --> 00:55:46.156
[MUSIC]

